From: surface-kit
Subject: [PATCH] software_isp: edge-preserving denoise pass; statistics before the GPU pass

At high gain (13x in a dim room) the rear camera image was grainy with
coloured speckles. The GPU debayer now renders into an intermediate RGB
texture and a second pass (denoise.frag) writes the output: luma smoothed
over 3x3 with weights from luma similarity, chroma averaged over 3x3 taps
4 pixels apart with a 3x wider tolerance, so colour speckles go but edges
and texture stay. 18 texture reads per pixel keep 3264x1836 at the
sensor's 20 fps on the Ice Lake GPU (a 5x5/5x5 version dropped to 18).

The strength comes from a new IPA algorithm, Denoise: sigma (output
noise at unity gain, 8-bit levels) times the square root of the gain,
capped at maxSigma. Picked on a textured wall at 13x: sigma 2.8 (10 at
13x). Without the algorithm the image is unchanged.

The statistics were taken after the GPU pass and its glFinish, so any GPU
work delayed every measurement autofocus pairs with a lens position: with
the denoise pass its sweeps shifted a step and missed. They only need the
raw input and are now taken first.

---
